                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the value below which a given percentage of the data lies. percentile 50 is the median: half the values are below it. percentile 90 has nine values in ten below it;\npercentile 0 is the smallest value and 100 the largest.\n\nPercentiles describe a spread of values without being thrown by a few wild ones. The mean of 1 to 9 plus one glitch of 100 is 14.5, a value none of the data is near; the median is 5.5, and percentile 90 is still only 18. Use them for a robust range (percentile 5 to 95 ignores the extremes), an automatic threshold (call the top 10% of activity \"loud\"), or to normalize a signal whose peaks are unreliable.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.percentile <percentile: float> <axis: int>\nDefaults: percentile 50, axis None (the whole array).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.percentile 90\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is measured and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\npercentile (float box, also an inlet):\nWhich percentile, 0 to 100 (values outside are clamped). Send it a list - 5 50 95 - to get several at once. It does not send by itself.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone takes the percentile over every element. A number takes it along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one value per column, 1 one per row.\n\nmethod:\nAn option, under the node. When the percentile falls between two values, linear (the default) blends them; lower, higher and nearest pick one of the two values;\nmidpoint takes their average.\n\nignore nan:\nAn option, under the node. Off, a single nan in the data makes the result nan. On, the nans are skipped.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe percentile: a single value, or one per percentile asked for, or an array of them along the axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: ten values, one of them a glitch. The mean is dragged up to 14.5; percentile 50 - the median - stays at 5.5 among the ordinary values.\n\nBottom: 1000 random values, evenly spread from 0 to 1. Percentile 90 comes out near 0.9, since nine values in ten are below that (round trims it to two decimal places for reading). The second node was sent 5 50 95 at\nload, so it gives three percentiles at once: about 0.05 0.5 0.95. The metro makes a new random array twice a second; the answers wobble a little each time.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.median is percentile 50. np.amin and np.amax are percentile 0 and 100. np.mean and np.std summarize the same data in a way that outliers do pull around;\nnp.robust_mean is a mean that resists them. np.histogram shows the whole spread."
        },
